    <string name="board_pinouts_board_uno_r3">The classic Arduino with an ATmega328P at 5 V and shield headers.</string>
    <string name="board_pinouts_board_nano">A breadboard-friendly ATmega328P board at 5 V. A6 and A7 are analog inputs only.</string>
    <string name="board_pinouts_board_pico_w">RP2040 board with Wi-Fi. GPIO is 3.3 V, and each signal can be routed to several pins.</string>
    <string name="board_pinouts_board_pico2_w">RP2350 board with Wi-Fi. Most GPIO tolerate 5 V while the chip is powered.</string>
    <string name="board_pinouts_board_rpi4b">The 40-pin header of the Raspberry Pi 4 Model B. GPIO is 3.3 V; extra buses need overlays.</string>
    <string name="board_pinouts_board_blue_pill">Low-cost STM32F103C8T6 board. Peripherals move between pins as whole groups (AFIO remap).</string>
    <string name="board_pinouts_board_black_pill">WeAct STM32F411CEU6 board. Each signal is routed to its own pin (AF mux).</string>
    <string name="board_pinouts_bnote_picovariant">Also for the Pico: same header. On the Pico, GP25 drives the LED, GP24 senses VBUS, GP23 controls the regulator and GP29 measures VSYS/3.</string>
    <string name="board_pinouts_bnote_pico2variant">Also for the Pico 2: same header. On the Pico 2, GP25 drives the LED, GP24 senses VBUS, GP23 controls the regulator and GP29 measures VSYS/3.</string>
    <string name="board_pinouts_bnote_f401variant">Black Pill boards with an STM32F401 share this header, but the F401 has fewer peripherals (no SPI5). Check its datasheet before planning.</string>
    <string name="board_pinouts_bnote_referenceschematic">This board has no official maker. The drawing follows the original vcc-gnd schematic published by stm32-base.org.</string>
    <string name="board_pinouts_bnote_clonesmaydiffer">Clones may use other onboard parts or values than the reference board.</string>
    <string name="board_pinouts_bnote_nofivevoltgpio">No GPIO on this header is 5 V tolerant. Apply 5 V only to the 5V power pins.</string>
    <string name="board_pinouts_bnote_usbpullupvalue">The USB D+ pull-up (PA12) on this board is 4.7 kΩ to 5 V instead of the 1.5 kΩ USB asks for; some hosts may not detect the board.</string>
    <string name="board_pinouts_bnote_fivevoltrailunprotected">The 5V pins connect straight to the USB connector without protection. Do not power the board from USB and an external supply at the same time.</string>
    <string name="board_pinouts_limit_fivevolttolerant">5 V tolerant</string>
    <string name="board_pinouts_limit_fivevolttolerant_explain">Accepts a 5 V input signal.</string>
    <string name="board_pinouts_limit_threevoltonly">3.3 V only</string>
    <string name="board_pinouts_limit_threevoltonly_explain">Not 5 V tolerant: a 5 V signal can damage the chip. Use a level shifter or a divider.</string>
